Let R = set of retrieved tokens (stopwords removed)
Let A = set of ground truth answer tokens
Let p = |R ∩ A| / |R|    (token precision ratio)

DEFINITION: PΩ = p²

THEOREM 1 (Boundedness):
  Since R ∩ A ⊆ R:
  0 ≤ |R ∩ A| ≤ |R|
  0 ≤ p ≤ 1
  0 ≤ p² ≤ 1
  Therefore 0 ≤ PΩ ≤ 1  □

THEOREM 2 (Noise sensitivity superiority):
  For any noisy retrieval where p < 1:
  d(p)/dp = 1         (Precision@K sensitivity — linear)
  d(p²)/dp = 2p       (PΩ sensitivity — quadratic)
  
  At p = 0.5: Precision@K = 0.5, PΩ = 0.25
  At p = 0.3: Precision@K = 0.3, PΩ = 0.09
  
  PΩ penalises noise quadratically.
  For p < 1, PΩ < Precision@K always.  □

THEOREM 3 (Extreme cases):
  Perfect retrieval (R = A): p = 1, PΩ = 1  ✓
  Complete noise (R ∩ A = ∅): p = 0, PΩ = 0  ✓  □

COROLLARY (Retrieval Paradox):
  Paradox exists when:
    Hit Rate = 1 (correct document present)
    AND PΩ < θ (content is mostly noise)
  From Theorem 2, this occurs when p < √θ
  For θ = 0.4: paradox when p < 0.632
  Meaning: even 63% token precision triggers paradox
  when standard metrics report full success.